      {
        "id": "p109-q82-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\ell$ is always tangential to $C$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $r>0$, $\\ell$ is tangent to $C$ at $P(\\alpha,\\beta)$.\n\nFor $\\beta\\ne0$, substitution gives $r^2x^2-2\\alpha r^2x+\\alpha^2r^2=0$, with discriminant $0$ and sole intersection $P$. For $\\beta=0$, the line is $x=\\alpha$ and its only intersection is $(\\alpha,0)=P$.",
          "worked": "Since $P$ lies on $C$, $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2=r^2>0$.\n\nFor $\\beta\\ne0$, substitute the expression for $y$ from $\\ell$ into $C$:\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\frac{r^2-\\alpha x}{\\beta}\\\\x^2+\\left(\\frac{r^2-\\alpha x}{\\beta}\\right)^2&=r^2\\\\\\beta^2x^2+(r^2-\\alpha x)^2&=\\beta^2r^2\\\\(\\alpha^2+\\beta^2)x^2-2\\alpha r^2x+r^4-\\beta^2r^2&=0\\\\r^2x^2-2\\alpha r^2x+\\alpha^2r^2&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThe last row uses $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2=r^2$ and $r^2-\\beta^2=\\alpha^2$. Its leading coefficient is nonzero, and its discriminant is\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(-2\\alpha r^2)^2-4(r^2)(\\alpha^2r^2)\\\\\\Delta&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThere is one repeated root. The intersection coordinates are\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\alpha\\\\y&=\\frac{r^2-\\alpha^2}{\\beta}\\\\y&=\\beta.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $\\beta=0$, $\\alpha^2=r^2>0$, so $\\alpha\\ne0$. The line $\\alpha x=r^2$ therefore gives $x=\\alpha$. Substitution into $C$ gives $y^2=0$, so $y=0$.\n\nIn both cases, the only intersection is $P(\\alpha,\\beta)$. Hence $\\ell$ is tangent to $C$ at $P$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 110
      },
      {
        "id": "p109-q82-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that $\\ell$ is always perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $r>0$, $\\ell\\perp OP$ at $P$, where $O=(0,0)$.\n\nFor $\\alpha\\beta\\ne0$, $m_{OP}m_\\ell=\\frac{\\beta}{\\alpha}\\left(-\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta}\\right)=-1$. At either axis point, one line is horizontal and the other is vertical.",
          "worked": "Let $O=(0,0)$. Part (a) gives the point of contact as $P(\\alpha,\\beta)$.\n\nFor $\\alpha\\beta\\ne0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}m_{OP}&=\\frac{\\beta}{\\alpha}\\\\m_\\ell&=-\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta}\\\\m_{OP}m_\\ell&=\\frac{\\beta}{\\alpha}\\left(-\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta}\\right)\\\\m_{OP}m_\\ell&=-1.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $OP$ and $\\ell$ are perpendicular.\n\nIf $\\alpha=0$, then $\\beta^2=r^2>0$. The radius $OP$ is vertical, and $\\ell$ reduces to the horizontal line $y=\\beta$.\n\nIf $\\beta=0$, then $\\alpha^2=r^2>0$. The radius $OP$ is horizontal, and $\\ell$ reduces to the vertical line $x=\\alpha$.\n\nThese cases exhaust the possibilities because $r>0$. Hence $\\ell$ is perpendicular to the radius $OP$ at the point of contact $P$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      }
    ]
  },
